[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 2. **Understand the folding process:**
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m    When the square is folded so that one pair of opposite corners coincide, the resulting triangle is congruent to one of the original triangles formed by the fold. The fold line is the perpendicular bisector of the line segment joining the opposite corners of the original square. This fold line is also the median of the original square, which is 7 inches long.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 3. **Calculate the area of one of the congruent triangles:**
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m    The area of one of the original triangles is half the area of the original square:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m    \text{Area of one triangle} = \frac{1}{2} \times 49 = 24.5 \text{ square inches}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 4. **Calculate the perimeter of one of the congruent triangles:**
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m    The perimeter of one of the congruent triangles is the sum of the lengths of its three sides. Since the fold line is the median, it divides the triangle into two congruent right triangles. Each of these right triangles has legs of length 7 inches (half the side length of the original square). The perimeter of one of these triangles is:
