[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m [response] To simplify the expression \(\sin^3 2x \cos 6x + \cos^3 2x \sin 6x\), we can use the identity for the product of sine and cosine functions. Specifically, we use the fact that \(\sin A \cos B = \frac{1}{2} [\sin (A+B) + \sin (A-B)]\). Applying this identity, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m \sin^3 2x \cos 6x + \cos^3 2x \sin 6x = \sin (2x + 6x) + \sin (2x - 6x) = \sin 8x + \sin (-4x) = \sin 8x - \sin 4x.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m So the original expression simplifies to:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
