[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m [response] To determine the number of vertical asymptotes of the function \( y = \frac{2}{x^2 + x - 6} \), we need to identify the values of \( x \) that make the denominator zero, as these are the points where the function is undefined and potentially has vertical asymptotes.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m First, we factor the denominator:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m \[ x^2 + x - 6 = (x + 3)(x - 2). \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m So, the function can be rewritten as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m \[ y = \frac{2}{(x + 3)(x - 2)}. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m The vertical asymptotes occur where the denominator is zero, i.e., at \( x = -3 \) and \( x = 2 \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=6911)[0m Therefore, the function has vertical asymptotes at \( x = -3 \) and \( x = 2 \). The number of vertical asymptotes is 2.
